# Coding Standards

## General Principles
- Follow SOLID principles and DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) patterns
- Use clear, semantic naming conventions
- Implement proper error handling with descriptive messages
- Write comprehensive documentation for all components and functions
- Ensure code is secure, performant, and accessible

## Code Style
- Use consistent indentation (2 spaces)
- Prefer functional programming patterns where appropriate
- Keep functions small and focused on a single responsibility
- Add helpful comments for complex logic, but avoid obvious comments
- Use meaningful variable and function names

## Security Practices
- Validate and sanitize all inputs
- Never expose sensitive information in client-side code
- Use prepared statements for database queries
- Implement proper authentication and authorization
- Follow OWASP security guidelines

## Performance
- Optimize database queries and data fetching
- Implement appropriate caching strategies
- Lazy load resources when possible
- Minimize HTTP requests
- Optimize asset delivery and loading

## Testing
- Write unit tests for all business logic
- Implement integration tests for critical workflows
- Aim for good test coverage for all components
- Use TDD (Test-Driven Development) when appropriate
- Test edge cases and error scenarios

## Version Control
- Write meaningful commit messages
- Use feature branches for development
- Perform code reviews for all changes
- Keep commits focused and atomic
- Follow conventional commit message format

## Documentation
- Document APIs with clear examples
- Maintain up-to-date README files
- Document setup instructions for new developers
- Keep documentation in sync with code changes

- Include usage examples for complex components